export const passages = {
 garden:{title:'A garden above the city',label:'Original text · community & sustainability',paragraphs:[
 'When the student council proposed a rooftop garden, most students imagined colourful flowers. Mariam, however, saw an opportunity to test an idea: could an unused school space become a place for both learning and food production? The principal approved a small trial, provided that an engineer confirmed the roof was safe and a teacher supervised the work.',
 'The team planted herbs in lightweight containers and used a drip system to water their roots. During the first month, several plants wilted. The students initially blamed the heat, but their records showed that the containers nearest the wall received too little water. After adjusting the system, they lost fewer plants. They learned that collecting evidence was more useful than defending their first guess.',
 'By the end of the term, the harvest was modest. It supplied herbs for two cooking lessons, not the entire school kitchen. Yet the garden had another effect: students who rarely spoke in class began explaining their measurements to visitors. The team decided to keep the trial small for another term so that they could compare water use before expanding.',
 'Mariam called the project a success, although it had not produced much food. For her, its value lay in the questions students learned to ask and the confidence they gained in answering them.'
 ]},
 repair:{title:'The Saturday repair table',label:'Original text · technology & community',paragraphs:[
 'Every second Saturday, volunteers set up a repair table in the neighbourhood library. Residents bring torn bags, broken lamps and other small items. The volunteers do not promise to repair everything. Instead, they inspect each item with its owner and explain what might be possible. Electrical items are handled only by a qualified volunteer.',
 'At first, Omar wanted to measure success by counting completed repairs. One morning, he spent an hour with an older resident whose radio needed a part that was no longer available. The radio left the library still silent, but the resident said that understanding the problem had helped her decide what to do next. Omar began to see that a useful visit did not always end with a repaired object.',
 'The group now records three things: whether an item was repaired, whether the owner learned a skill, and whether a further action was agreed. These records do not prove how much waste the whole neighbourhood has avoided. They describe only the items brought to the table. The volunteers are careful about this limit when they share their results.',
 'The library has offered them a larger room, but the group will accept only if more qualified volunteers join. A crowded table might look impressive, Omar says, yet giving each visitor enough time matters more than attracting a large queue.'
 ]}
};

