export const writingPrompts = [
 {id:'opinion',title:'A little time. A bigger difference.',genre:'Opinion essay',min:180,max:220,prompt:'Should schools offer a weekly volunteering session? Write an opinion essay for your school magazine. State your position, develop two reasons, and address one possible concern.',guidance:['Plan an introduction, two body paragraphs and a conclusion.','Use a relevant example and explain how it supports your point.','Acknowledge a concern and respond respectfully.','Use hypothetical examples honestly; do not invent research findings.'],model:'Schools should offer a weekly volunteering session because it can build practical skills and strengthen relationships within the community. The session should be carefully planned so that students can take part without losing essential study time.\n\nFirst, volunteering gives students a reason to use skills beyond the classroom. For example, a group organising donated books might need to divide tasks, keep records and explain its system to others. These activities provide practice in communication and responsibility. They also allow students with different strengths to contribute.\n\nSecond, working on a shared task can help students understand local needs. A library project, for instance, may show them why accessible reading spaces matter. However, some students may worry that volunteering reduces revision time. Schools could respond by keeping sessions short and offering flexible roles during examination periods.\n\nIn conclusion, a weekly session could be valuable if it has a clear purpose and a realistic schedule. Schools should begin with a small trial, ask students and community partners for feedback, and adjust the programme before expanding it. This approach would make volunteering a meaningful part of education rather than an extra task to complete.'},
 {id:'proposal',title:'Make space for better learning.',genre:'Formal proposal email',min:150,max:190,prompt:'Write an email to your principal proposing a peer study club. Explain its purpose, give a practical plan, and suggest how the school could evaluate a four-week trial.',guidance:['Use a respectful greeting and closing.','State the purpose in your opening sentence.','Include a place, schedule and supervision plan as proposals.','Suggest a realistic way to collect feedback after the trial.'],model:'Dear Principal,\n\nI am writing to propose a peer study club for Grade 12 students. The club would give students a regular opportunity to discuss difficult topics and practise explaining ideas to one another.\n\nWith your permission, we could run a four-week trial in the library on Wednesday afternoons. Each meeting would last thirty minutes, and we would ask a teacher to supervise. Students could choose a subject group and bring one question to discuss. Volunteer group leaders would help everyone participate, but they would refer questions they could not answer to the teacher.\n\nTo evaluate the trial, we could record attendance and ask participants to complete a short anonymous feedback form. The form would ask whether the sessions were useful and what should change. These responses would help us improve the club before deciding whether to continue.\n\nThank you for considering this proposal. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss the plan with you.\n\nYours sincerely,\nA Grade 12 student'}
];
